{:edb/db-before-t 1703 :edb/db-after-t 1704 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535016 50 #inst "2026-10-08T14:42:31.652Z" 13194139535016 true] [17592186179458 1100 "The half-life of bismuth-210 is $5.012$ days. How long does it take for a $1\\,280\\,\\textrm g$ sample of bismuth-210 to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g$?" 13194139535016 false] [17592186179458 1100 "The half-life of bismuth-210 is $5.012$ days. How long does it take for a $1\\,280\\,\\textrm g$ sample of bismuth-210 to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g?$" 13194139535016 true] [17592186179458 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = 5.012\n$$\n\ndays we have\n\n$$\n5.012 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{5.012}\n$$\n\nAlso, since\n\n$$\nM_0 = 1\\,280\\,\\textrm g\n$$\n\nwe have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t) = 1\\,280 e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that\n\n$$\nM(t) = 5\\,\\textrm g\n$$\n\nSo, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n5 = 1\\,280 e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= 1280e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{1280} &= e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{256} &= e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012} \\\\\n\\ln (\\frac{1}{256}) &= \\ln (e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}) \\\\\n\\ln 1 - \\ln 256 &= -\\frac{t\\ln 2}{5.012} \\\\\n- \\ln 256 &= -\\frac{t\\ln 2}{5.012} \\\\\nt &= \\frac{5.012 \\cdot \\ln 256}{\\ln 2} \\\\\n&= t \\mid \\approx 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $40$ days for the mass of the substance to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that\n\n$$\nM(t) = 5\\,\\textrm g\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(M(t))/(M(0)) &= \\frac{5g}{1280g} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{256} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2})^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^8\\cdot M(0)\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $8$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $1\\,280\\,\\textrm g$ to $5\\,\\textrm g$. Hence,\n\n$$\nt = 8\\cdot t_{1/2} =8 \\cdot 5.012 \\approx 40\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $40$ days for the mass of the substance to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g$." 13194139535016 false] [17592186179458 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that $t_{1/2} = 5.012$ days we have\n\n$$\n5.012 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{5.012}\n$$\n\nAlso, since $M_0 = 1\\,280\\,\\textrm g,$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t) = 1\\,280 e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t) = 5\\,\\textrm g.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n5 = 1\\,280 e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =1\\,280{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.012} \\\\ \\frac{5}{1\\,280} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.012} \\\\ \\frac{1}{256} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.012} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{256}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.012}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡256 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.012} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡256 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.012} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.012\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡256}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $40$ days for the mass of the substance to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t) = 5\\,\\textrm g.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{5\\,\\text{g}}{1\\,280\\,\\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{256} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t) =  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^8\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $8$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $1\\,280\\,\\textrm g$ to $5\\,\\textrm g.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt = 8\\cdot t_{1/2} =8 \\cdot 5.012 \\approx 40.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $40$ days for the mass of the substance to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g.$" 13194139535016 true] [17592186179486 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\n$$\n\nSince we know that\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = 301\n$$\n\nthousand years, we have\n\n$$\n301 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{301}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that\n\n$$\nM(t) = 0.65 M_0\n$$\n\nSo, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.65M_0 = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.65M_{0} &= M_{0}e^{-t\\ln 2/301} \\\\\n0.65M_{0} &= M_{0}e^{-t\\ln 2/301} \\\\\n0.65 &= e^{-t\\ln 2/301} \\\\\n\\ln (0.65) &= \\ln (e^{-t\\ln 2/301}) \\\\\n\\ln (0.65) &= -\\frac{t\\ln 2}{301} \\\\\nt &= - (301 \\cdot \\ln (0.65))/(\\ln 2) \\\\\n&= t \\mid \\approx 187\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $187$ thousand years old." 13194139535016 false] [17592186179486 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that $t_{1/2} = 301$ thousand years, we have\n\n$$\n301 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{301}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t) = 0.65 M_0.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.65M_0 = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.65{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ 0.65\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ 0.65 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.65) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.65) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{301} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{301\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.65)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈187.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $187$ thousand years old." 13194139535016 true] [17592186179487 1100 "The mass $M(t)$, in grams, of a sample of iron-60 is given by\n\n$M(t) = 100 e^{-kt}$,\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in millions of years. The half-life of iron-60 is $2.6$ million years. Rounded to three significant figures, what is the value of $k$?" 13194139535016 false] [17592186179487 1100 "The mass $M(t),$ in grams, of a sample of iron-60 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t) = 100 e^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in millions of years. The half-life of iron-60 is $2.6$ million years. Rounded to three significant figures, what is the value of $k?$" 13194139535016 true] [17592186179487 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\n$$\n\nSince we know that\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = 2.6\n$$\n\nmillion years, we have\n\n$$\n2.6 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{2.6} \\approx 0.267\n$$" 13194139535016 false] [17592186179487 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that $t_{1/2} = 2.6$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n2.6 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{2.6} \\approx 0.267.\n$$" 13194139535016 true] [17592186179736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326969 13194139535016 true] [17592186179736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326976 13194139535016 true] [17592186179736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327004 13194139535016 true] [17592186179736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327025 13194139535016 true] [17592186179736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327047 13194139535016 true] [17592186179737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326983 13194139535016 true] [17592186179737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327011 13194139535016 true] [17592186179737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327032 13194139535016 true] [17592186179737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327035 13194139535016 t