fe ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $k=0.025\\,67.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1/2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.025\\,67} \\\\  & ≈27.0\\,\\text{days}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326977 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326978 13194139535016 true] [17592186326977 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326979 13194139535016 true] [17592186326977 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326980 13194139535016 true] [17592186326977 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326981 13194139535016 true] [17592186326977 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326982 13194139535016 true] [17592186326977 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535016 true] [17592186326977 1041 "selection" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326977 1104 #edb/ref 17592186326982 13194139535016 true] [17592186326977 1110 #uuid "0709e52b-f165-5efc-a44f-7a037e104f52" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326978 1034 "$27.8$ days" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326978 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535016 true] [17592186326978 1111 #uuid "d8313d91-f16c-515b-b254-b4a486a6a738" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326979 1034 "$27.4$ days" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326979 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535016 true] [17592186326979 1111 #uuid "a5000016-075b-5afc-a3bf-4ffe1bef92d3" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326980 1034 "$25.2$ days" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326980 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535016 true] [17592186326980 1111 #uuid "225c6fe3-d979-5a79-ab39-fe1eac10fdf9" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326981 1034 "$26.6$ days" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326981 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535016 true] [17592186326981 1111 #uuid "8e147f5a-f20d-55c6-98ec-7813403870a5" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326982 1034 "$27.0$ days" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326982 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535016 true] [17592186326982 1111 #uuid "0a0a40ce-91b2-5703-8b92-73e73a4221d2" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326983 1017 #edb/ref 17592186326984 13194139535016 true] [17592186326983 1089 "q-118602" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326983 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535016 true] [17592186326983 1100 "The half-life of francium-224 is $3.33\\,\\text{min}.$ Rounded to the nearest minute, how long does it take for a $160\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of francium-224 to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326983 1113 #uuid "ffb198e3-2319-570d-8d3c-85ada19388e6" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326983 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=3.33\\,\\text{min},$ we have\n\n$$\n3.33=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=160\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n20=160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\frac{20}{160} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33} \\\\ t & =\\frac{3.33\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $10\\,\\text{min}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{20\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{160\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $160\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 3.33\\,\\text{min}≈10\\,\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $10\\,\\text{min}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326984 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326985 13194139535016 true] [17592186326984 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326986 13194139535016 true] [17592186326984 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326987 13194139535016 true] [17592186326984 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326988 13194139535016 true] [17592186326984 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326989 13194139535016 true] [17592186326984 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535016 true] [17592186326984 1041 "selection" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326984 1104 #edb/ref 17592186326987 13194139535016 true] [17592186326984 1110 #uuid "cca8d2c4-fe28-5450-b1d2-e3ddd9c51257" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326985 1034 "13\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326985 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535016 true] [17592186326985 1111 #uuid "bbc6c043-aaa7-55b5-ac8e-d13cd27f7493" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326986 1034 "15\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326986 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535016 true] [17592186326986 1111 #uuid "92f21cae-e848-50fd-b3a2-4f53911cc81a" 13194139535016 true] [17592186326987