] [17592186327035 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535016 true] [17592186327035 1100 "Iodine-131 has a half-life of $8.02$ days. A sample of iodine-131 has an initial mass of $7.2\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Rounded to **one decimal place**, what is the mass of the sample after $12$ days?\n\nThe mass of the sample after $12$ days is approximately\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mu \\text{g}.$" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327035 1113 #uuid "ff8bfa72-3204-5df3-86a4-a6840e17f1a6" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327035 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=8.02$ days, we have\n\n$$\n8.02=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{8.02}.\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=7.2\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=7.2{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/8.02}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nM(12)=7.2{e}^{-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/8.02}≈2.6,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the mass of the sample remaining after $12$ days is approximately $2.6\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327037 13194139535016 true] [17592186327036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327038 13194139535016 true] [17592186327036 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535016 true] [17592186327036 1041 "field-1" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327036 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327037 13194139535016 true] [17592186327036 1110 #uuid "3777cd74-5ece-546f-af31-113e85e507a4" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327037 1034 "2.6" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327037 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535016 true] [17592186327037 1111 #uuid "a0e4cb60-891b-57ed-83fe-ff500fbd13da" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327038 1034 "3.6" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327038 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535016 true] [17592186327038 1111 #uuid "94543470-7985-52d5-8828-111975cf3dbd" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327039 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327040 13194139535016 true] [17592186327039 1089 "q-353546" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327039 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535016 true] [17592186327039 1100 "The half-life of polonium-210 is $138.4$ days. How long does it take for a $8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of polonium-210 to reduce to $1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{days}$" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327039 1113 #uuid "7f844e0a-d105-5cbd-ae82-24250701f497" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327039 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=138.4$ days, we have\n\n$$\n138.4=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.1 & =8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.1 & =8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\frac{1.1}{8.8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4} \\\\ t & =\\frac{138.4\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{138.4\\cdot 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =415.2\\,\\text{days}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 138.4=415.2\\,\\text{days}.\n$$" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327040 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327041 13194139535016 true] [17592186327040 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327042 13194139535016 true] [17592186327040 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535016 true] [17592186327040 1041 "field-1" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327040 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327042 13194139535016 true] [17592186327040 1110 #uuid "97a41f62-c155-5397-8795-7d9044a01696" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327041 1034 "276.8" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327041 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535016 true] [17592186327041 1111 #uuid "17ca6d1f-d70a-55e9-a3c5-ef3c77fec547" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327042 1034 "415.2" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327042 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535016 true] [17592186327042 1111 #uuid "be4ffe4f-7ae7-5f86-8417-329f40a99bc2" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327043 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327044 13194139535016 true] [17592186327043 1089 "q-353610" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327043 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535016 true] [17592186327043 1100 "In a medical imaging facility, a tracer is found with $25%$ of its original sodium-22 content. Given that the half-life of sodium-22 is $2.6$ years, how old is the tracer? Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\nThe tracer is approximately\n{{field-1}}\nyears old." 13194139535016 true] [17592186327043 1113 #uuid "db8660ee-2d08-5e8e-b776-b18ced9354e2" 13194139535016 true] [17592186327043 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=2.6$ years, we have\n\n$$\n2.6=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.6}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.25{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.25{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.25{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ 0.25\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ 0.25 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25) & =ln\\,({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.6} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2.6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈5.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the tracer is approximately $5.2$ years old." 13194139535016 true] [17592186327044 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327045 13194139535016 true] [17592186327044 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327046 13194139535016 true] [17592186327044 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 131941